---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Robot Vacuums",
    "url": "/best-robot-vacuum/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Robot Vacuum vs Regular Vacuum",
    "url": "/robot-vacuum-vs-regular-vacuum/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Do Robot Vacuums Work on Pet Hair?",
    "url": "/do-robot-vacuums-work-on-pet-hair/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Shop Vacs",
    "url": "/best-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Vacuum Water With a Shop Vac",
    "url": "/how-to-vacuum-water-with-a-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Food Dehydrators",
    "url": "/best-food-dehydrator/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-19", modified: "2026-10-19" });
const title = "7 Best Carpet Cleaners: Upright, Portable and Pet Picks for Every Home";
const description =
  "The best carpet cleaners for whole rooms, pet stains and stairs, from heated uprights to portable spot cleaners, compared on tank size, hose, cord and warranty.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "bissell-revolution-hydrosteam-pet",
    "name": "Bissell Revolution HydroSteam Pet Carpet Cleaner (3432)",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Heated Upright Carpet Cleaner",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Revolution HydroSteam Pet is the upright we would point most households toward, because it pairs the two things that decide how a carpet looks afterward: aggressive scrubbing and strong water recovery. Bissell lists two brush rolls, each with 12 rows of bristles, and describes HydroSteam as a heater and fluid delivery system that combines steam vapor, water and cleaning solution. On Bissell's product page the machine carries a $399.99 list price, a 25-foot cord, an 8-foot hose, an 11-inch cleaning path, a 12-amp motor and a weight of 22.5 pounds. Bissell also advertises a Quick Dry mode that it says can dry carpet in 30 minutes, with a footnote that the claim was measured on new carpet with protectant and that results vary, so treat that as a best case rather than a promise for old, matted carpet. Tank capacity is the one spec the sources describe inconsistently: Bissell's page lists a 1-gallon dirty tank, and Sam's Club describes a 1-gallon clean tank. Either way, plan on refilling a couple of times for a large living room. The included tools vary by listing; Bissell lists a pet upholstery tool and a tough stain tool, and its page is inconsistent about which stair tool comes in the box. Retail listings and Bissell's page describe a 5-year limited warranty.",
    "pros": [
      "Two 12-row brush rolls for heavy agitation, per Bissell",
      "Heated cleaning via HydroSteam",
      "25 ft cord and 8 ft hose for whole-room reach",
      "Quick Dry mode, with Bissell's own caveats on the 30-minute claim",
      "5-year limited warranty listed"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "$399.99 list price is high for occasional use",
      "At 22.5 lb it is heavy on stairs",
      "Tank capacity described inconsistently between listings"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a home that deep-cleans carpet a few times a year and wants the best chance of lifting set-in dirt, the HydroSteam Pet is the upright to buy."
  },
  {
    "id": "bissell-big-green-86t3",
    "name": "Bissell Big Green Deep Cleaning Machine (86T3)",
    "badge": "Best for Heavy Soil",
    "category": "Large-Tank Upright",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Big Green is the closest thing to a rental machine you can keep in your own garage. It is built around capacity and reach rather than convenience: retailers list a 1.75-gallon tank, a 25-foot cord, a 9-foot hose and a 10.5-inch cleaning path, with Best Buy and Target listing it at about 42 pounds and Sylvane at 40.6 pounds. That weight is the trade-off. It is a machine for a whole floor of carpet, a long hallway or a rental move-out, not for a quick pass over a coffee spill. The bigger tank means fewer trips to the sink on a large job, and the long hose reaches stairs and upholstery without dragging the base along. Target and Sylvane list a 5-year limited warranty. Availability is the thing to check before you fall for it: Best Buy currently shows the 86T3 as no longer available new, so look at other retailers and confirm the exact model number. If you rent a machine more than twice a year, the Big Green tends to pay for itself, because you are no longer paying rental fees and buying the rental company's solution at its price.",
    "pros": [
      "1.75-gallon tank for large jobs",
      "25 ft cord and 9 ft hose",
      "Rental-style build for heavy soil",
      "5-year limited warranty listed"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 40 to 42 lb, depending on the listing",
      "Bulky to store",
      "Shown as unavailable new at Best Buy; check stock"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy the Big Green if you clean a lot of carpet at once and were going to rent a machine anyway."
  },
  {
    "id": "bissell-proheat-2x-revolution-pet-pro",
    "name": "Bissell ProHeat 2X Revolution Pet Pro (2007F / 1986 family)",
    "badge": "Best Value Upright",
    "category": "Mid-Range Upright",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The ProHeat 2X Revolution Pet Pro family is the sensible middle of the range: most of what the premium uprights do, for noticeably less money. Bissell's page for the 1986 model lists a 25-foot cord, a 6.8-amp motor, a 1-gallon clean water tank, an 8-foot hose, an 11-inch cleaning path and dual DirtLifter PowerBrushes with 12 rows. A Costco listing for the closely related 2007F gives the same 25-foot cord, 11-inch path and 1-gallon clean tank, with a total weight of 17.5 pounds, and it mentions a 10-foot hose rather than 8, so check the box for the exact length. Bissell describes Heatwave technology as working with the brush rolls, but its 1986 page does not list a tank heater specification, and a reseller listing marks the model as having an integrated heater. The 1986 is listed as discontinued in places, with the 2007F still sold at Costco at $219.99 for members, which is the reference price we would use. The lower weight is the real-world advantage over the Big Green and the HydroSteam: at about 17.5 pounds it is far easier to carry up a flight of stairs.",
    "pros": [
      "1-gallon clean tank and 25 ft cord",
      "Dual 12-row brush rolls, per Bissell",
      "About 17.5 lb per the Costco listing",
      "Costco price of $219.99 is good value"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Several model numbers in circulation; check yours",
      "Heater spec not stated on Bissell's 1986 page",
      "Hose listed at 8 ft or 10 ft depending on source"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The ProHeat 2X Pet Pro is the upright to buy if you want strong scrubbing without paying for the flagship."
  },
  {
    "id": "hoover-smartwash-pet-complete",
    "name": "Hoover SmartWash Pet Complete (FH53000 family)",
    "badge": "Easiest to Use",
    "category": "Automatic Upright",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Hoover's SmartWash is built around one idea: take the guesswork out of how much solution to put down. The machine mixes and dispenses automatically as you push it forward and switches to suction as you pull back, which is the part most first-time users get wrong with a manual-trigger machine. A distributor listing for the SmartWash Pet Complete gives a 3.79-liter fresh water tank and a 1.89-liter dirty tank, a 96-inch hose and a weight of 18.9 pounds, and an eBay listing names the FH53000PC model at 2000 watts. The Brick lists a 22-foot cord and a 5-year motor warranty. Model numbers are messy here, too: Sylvane lists the FH5300FS (FH53000) as discontinued, and Hoover's own site lists an FH53050 variant, so treat the specs as belonging to the family rather than one SKU, and confirm against the manual of the unit you are actually buying. The automatic dispensing is the reason to choose it. If you have never used a carpet cleaner and want a machine that is hard to over-wet carpet with, this is it.",
    "pros": [
      "Automatic solution mixing and dispensing",
      "About 1-gallon fresh tank per a distributor listing",
      "5-year motor warranty listed by The Brick",
      "About 18.9 lb"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Specs come from distributor and reseller listings",
      "Several model numbers in circulation",
      "Dirty tank is smaller than the fresh tank"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Choose the SmartWash if you want a machine that makes the right decisions for you about how much water and solution to use."
  },
  {
    "id": "hoover-powerdash-pet",
    "name": "Hoover PowerDash Pet (FH50700)",
    "badge": "Best Budget Upright",
    "category": "Compact Upright",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The PowerDash Pet is the cheapest way into a real upright carpet cleaner, and the compromises are the ones you would expect. Listings give a 1.89-liter fresh water tank, about half a gallon, and a 1.51-liter dirty tank, an 840-watt motor on 120 volts, a 20-foot cord and a 10-inch brush. Weight varies by listing, from about 12.5 to 13 pounds on most to 16.2 pounds at Walmart, which may include packaging. Distributor pricing runs from about $101 to an MSRP of $119.99, and Walmart lists a close sibling, the FH50710CN, at $108. The small tank is the main drawback: you will refill often on anything bigger than a bedroom. The upside of that small tank is a light machine that is easy to carry upstairs and store in a closet. The Brick lists a 1-year manufacturer's warranty, which is notably shorter than the 5 years on the Bissell uprights above. For a renter, a small apartment or occasional pet cleanups on a single floor, it is plenty of machine.",
    "pros": [
      "About $100 to $120 at most retailers",
      "Light, roughly 12.5 to 13 lb on most listings",
      "Retractable 20 ft cord",
      "Small enough for closet storage"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Half-gallon fresh tank means frequent refills",
      "1-year warranty per The Brick",
      "Weight figures disagree between listings"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy the PowerDash Pet for small spaces and occasional use; step up if you clean whole floors."
  },
  {
    "id": "bissell-little-green-pro-2505",
    "name": "Bissell Little Green Pro (2505)",
    "badge": "Best Portable",
    "category": "Portable Spot Cleaner",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Every home with a pet or a toddler eventually needs a spot cleaner, and the Little Green Pro is the one we would buy for stairs, upholstery and car seats. Bissell's Canadian page for the 2505 lists a 2.8-liter tank, a 5-foot hose and a 20-foot cord, and Costco's listing gives the same 20-foot cord, a 2.84-liter (3-quart) water capacity and a weight of about 13 pounds. That bigger tank is the reason to choose it over the smaller Little Green models: you can do a full staircase without stopping. Pricing swings widely, from a Walmart listing at $157 to a SheKnows-reported sale at $109, so watch for deals. Be careful with the model number, though. Canadian Tire lists other Little Green Pro variants with a 0.5-gallon tank and a 4.92-foot hose, and the commercial BGSS1481 is a different machine again. We did not find a clear statement of the US warranty term for the 2505, so confirm it with Bissell before buying.",
    "pros": [
      "About 3-quart tank for a portable",
      "20 ft cord and 5 ft hose",
      "About 13 lb, easy to carry up stairs",
      "Frequently on sale"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Warranty term not clearly stated for the US 2505",
      "Several Little Green Pro variants with different specs",
      "Not a whole-room machine"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Little Green Pro is the portable to buy if you clean stairs, furniture or vehicles more than the occasional spill."
  },
  {
    "id": "bissell-spotclean-pro-3624",
    "name": "Bissell SpotClean Pro (3624)",
    "badge": "Best Value Portable",
    "category": "Portable Spot Cleaner",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The SpotClean Pro is the long-running workhorse of portable carpet cleaners, and it remains a sound pick when you can find it at a good price. Bissell's page lists a 5-foot hose and a 22-foot cord, retailers describe a three-quarter-gallon solution tank, and Newegg lists 5.7 amps, no heater, about 13.2 pounds, and a 3-inch tough stain tool plus a 6-inch stair tool. Walmart has shown it at $99.99, but both Walmart and Newegg listed it as out of stock, so availability is the thing to check. The larger tank gives it the same advantage as the Little Green Pro over smaller spot cleaners. It does not heat the water, so if you want warmth you are relying on starting with hot tap water. For pet owners, the stair tool and the long cord make it easy to work up a staircase from one outlet.",
    "pros": [
      "Three-quarter-gallon tank",
      "22 ft cord and 5 ft hose",
      "Stair and stain tools included",
      "About 13.2 lb"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No heater",
      "Often out of stock at major retailers",
      "Price has been around $100, check for the latest"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy the SpotClean Pro if you find it in stock near $100; otherwise the Little Green Pro does the same job."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Carpet Cleaners",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-carpet-cleaner/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best carpet cleaner for most homes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes with wall-to-wall carpet, a heated upright with a large tank and long cord is the best choice, such as the Bissell Revolution HydroSteam Pet. If your budget is tighter, the ProHeat 2X Revolution Pet Pro family offers similar scrubbing for less money. Pair either with a portable for stairs and spills."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it better to buy or rent a carpet cleaner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you deep-clean carpet more than once a year, or you have pets that cause frequent messes, owning usually costs less than renting over time and lets you treat stains immediately. If you clean once every few years, renting a large machine can make sense. Professional cleaning is best for heavily soiled or warrantied carpet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does carpet take to dry after cleaning?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the carpet, the amount of water used and the room's humidity. Carpet is often dry to the touch in a few hours and fully dry within about a day. Bissell advertises a 30-minute Quick Dry mode on the HydroSteam Pet but notes results vary. Extra dry passes, fans and a dehumidifier shorten drying."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a carpet cleaner on upholstery?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually yes, with the hose and an upholstery tool, but check the fabric's care code first. Many upholstered pieces are labeled for water-based cleaning, but some are solvent-only or dry-clean only. Test a hidden area, use as little water as possible and extract thoroughly so the cushion fill does not stay damp."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do carpet cleaners remove pet urine smell?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They can, if the urine has not soaked deep into the pad. Treat the area with an enzyme cleaner designed for pet urine, let it dwell as directed, then extract slowly with several dry passes. If odor returns after cleaning, the pad underneath may be contaminated and need replacing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a carpet cleaner and a steam cleaner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most home carpet cleaners are hot-water extractors: they spray warm water and solution and suck it back out. Some, like Bissell's HydroSteam, add a heater to warm the solution. Neither reaches the temperatures of a professional truck-mounted system, and a hard-floor steam mop is a different tool that is not designed for carpet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I deep-clean my carpet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many carpet manufacturers suggest professional or deep cleaning about every 12 to 18 months, more often in homes with pets, kids or heavy traffic. Check your carpet warranty for its specific requirement. Between deep cleans, vacuum regularly and treat spills quickly with a portable spot cleaner."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Appliances",
    tags: [
      "best carpet cleaner",
      "carpet cleaner machine",
      "best carpet shampooer",
      "best carpet cleaner for pets",
      "portable carpet cleaner",
      "upright carpet cleaner"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Carpet Care
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Carpet Cleaners <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">for Whole Rooms, Pets and Stairs</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven carpet cleaners chosen by the job they are bought for, because a machine that deep-cleans a whole living room is the wrong tool for a single stain on the stairs.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#bissell-revolution-hydrosteam-pet"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Upright or Portable?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="14.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#upright-vs-portable" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Upright vs Portable</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#specs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Specs That Matter</a>
          <a href="#heat" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Heat, Steam and Dry Time</a>
          <a href="#pets" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Carpet Cleaners for Pets</a>
          <a href="#how-to-use" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Get Pro-Level Results</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Owning vs Renting vs a Pro</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Keeping the Machine Clean</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best carpet cleaner is the one sized to the mess you actually have. Whole-room cleaning needs an upright with a big tank, a long cord and strong suction; pet accidents and stairs need a portable spot cleaner you will actually pull out of the closet. Most homes with pets end up owning one of each, and that is not a waste of money.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carpet cleaners are an easy category to buy badly, because two very different machines share the same name. An upright carpet cleaner is a full-width deep cleaner you push around a room like a vacuum. A portable carpet cleaner, often called a spot cleaner, is a small canister with a hose and hand tool that you carry to a stain. Both spray water and solution into the fibers and suck the dirty water back out, but they solve different problems, and buying the wrong one is the most common reason people give up on owning a machine at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            So we chose by job. For whole rooms we looked for big tanks, long cords, heated cleaning, strong brush agitation and good water recovery. For spots and stairs we looked for tank size, hose length and weight, because a portable that runs dry halfway up a staircase or is too heavy to lift is a portable that stays in the closet. Every specification on this page comes from Bissell or Hoover, from retailer listings or from owner manuals, and we tell you where sources disagree rather than picking the friendliest number.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these machines, and we do not publish cleaning scores. What we can do is read the specifications closely, compare them against what the job demands, and flag the gotchas that matter: model numbers that change between retailers, warranties that differ by a factor of five, tank sizes listed differently by different sellers, and machines that are drifting out of stock. That is the information you need before you spend $100 to $400 on a machine you will use a few times a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most homes are best served by one upright and one portable. If you can only buy one, buy the upright if you have a lot of wall-to-wall carpet, and the portable if you mostly have hard floors with a few rugs, upholstered furniture and a pet that has accidents.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upright-vs-portable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Upright vs Portable Carpet Cleaners
          </h2>

          <p>
            An upright carpet cleaner is the machine for cleaning a whole room. It has a cleaning path of roughly 10 to 11 inches on the models here, motorized brush rolls that scrub the fibers, a clean water tank and a separate dirty water tank, and usually a hose and a few tools for stairs and upholstery. It is the right tool when the whole carpet looks dull, after a party, before you sell a house, or as part of a once-or-twice-a-year deep clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            A portable carpet cleaner is the machine for stains. It is a small canister you carry to the mess, with a hose and a hand tool that sprays and extracts. It is also the better tool for anything an upright cannot reach easily: stairs, upholstered sofas and chairs, car seats, and the pet accident on a rug in the hallway. Portables are slower over a large area, but they are faster to get out, which means they actually get used.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which one first?</h3>

          <p>
            If you have wall-to-wall carpet in several rooms, start with an upright. If you mostly have hard floors with area rugs, or if pets and kids create frequent small messes, start with a portable. Many owners eventually buy both, and that is a reasonable outcome rather than overspending, because the two machines overlap less than their names suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            One middle ground is worth knowing about. Most uprights come with a hose and a hand tool, so they can do some spot cleaning. In practice, though, hauling out a 17- to 42-pound upright and filling its tank for a single stain is enough friction that many people simply do not do it. A dedicated portable costs between about $100 and $160 on the models here, which is modest insurance for a home with pets.
          </p>
        </div>

